// vader/lower/opt_report — where an optimisation gave up, and on what.
//
// The optimisation passes under `lower/` are SHAPE RECOGNISERS: each tries to
// match a form, and returns `null` when it doesn't, falling through to a
// general path that is correct but slower. There are 304 such bail-outs, and
// not one of them says so — so a program can miss every fast path in the
// compiler and look, from the outside, exactly like one that hits them all.
//
// This records the FALL, not the refusal. A pass declines many times on its way
// to a decision (`lower_iter_fusion` alone has 40 refusal points); what a reader
// needs is the single place where it has exhausted its attempts and the general
// path takes over. That is ~10 sites instead of 304, and it answers the question
// the refusals cannot: how often does a real program miss this, and where.
//
// Inert unless `$VADER_OPT_REPORT` is set — the env is read once per lowering
// run and cached on `LowerProjectCtx`, so a disabled report costs one bool test
// at each fall site.
//
// NOT a diagnostic. It never reaches `Diagnostic[]`, never gates a build, and
// never changes emitted code. Missing an optimisation is not a user error; this
// is an instrument, and treating it as a warning would push people to rewrite
// working code to silence it.
//
// ⚠️ COUNT THE STATE, NOT THE ATTEMPT. A pass can miss in two ways: it tried and
// refused, or it was never reached at all. Instrumenting only the first leaves a
// blind spot — `bench/arr_rw` walks `for i in 0..<n` with `n` a separate local,
// so no `arr.len()` bound exists to pair with the loop, `bh_try_hoist` never
// runs, and the report stays silent on a loop that is documented as costing
// 1.6x against Go. A site added here should ask "does this construct END UP on
// the slow path", not "did a recogniser decline". The `for-in` fall site does
// this correctly (it sits after every attempt); the BCE one does not yet, and
// says so in its own note.

module "vader/lower"

import "std/collections"
import "std/env"
import "std/string"
import "std/string_builder"
import "toolchain/span"

/// One point where an optimisation pass handed over to the general path.
export OptMiss :: struct {
    /// The pass that gave up, in the words a reader would use — "for-in fusion",
    /// not `try_lower_chain_fusion`.
    pass: string
    /// The source construct that missed it.
    span: Span
    /// What happens instead, and the property that would have been needed.
    /// Written for someone deciding whether to care, not for whoever wrote
    /// the pass.
    note: string
}

/// Environment variable that turns the report on.
export OPT_REPORT_VAR :: "VADER_OPT_REPORT"

/// Whether `$VADER_OPT_REPORT` is set to something other than `0`.
///
/// Read once per lowering run — `record_miss` consults the cached flag, so the
/// per-site cost when off is a bool test rather than an environment lookup.
export opt_report_enabled :: fn() -> bool {
    raw :: get_env(OPT_REPORT_VAR) ?? return false
    return raw != "" && raw != "0"
}

/// Note that `pass` fell back to its general path at `span`.
///
/// A no-op unless the report is on. Callers sit at the single fall site of a
/// pass, never at its individual refusals.
///
/// @param ctx   the lowering project context, which accumulates the misses.
/// @param pass  the pass, named for a reader.
/// @param note  what runs instead, and what would have been needed.
export record_miss :: fn(ctx: LowerProjectCtx!, pass: string, span: Span, note: string) -> void {
    if !ctx.opt_report {
        return
    }
    ctx.opt_misses.push(OptMiss { .pass = pass, .span = span, .note = note })
}

/// Render the collected misses, grouped by pass and ordered by how often each
/// fired — the frequent ones are the ones worth acting on.
///
/// Returns "" when the report is off or nothing missed, so the caller can print
/// unconditionally.
export render_opt_report :: fn(ctx: LowerProjectCtx) -> string {
    if ctx.opt_misses.len() == 0 {
        return ""
    }
    counts :: MutableMap<string, i32> {}
    for m in ctx.opt_misses {
        counts[m.pass] = (counts[m.pass] ?? 0) + 1
    }
    out :: StringBuilder {}
    out.append("\n[VADER_OPT_REPORT] optimisations that handed over to the general path\n")
    for [pass, n] in counts {
        out.append("  ${pass}: ${n}\n")
    }
    out.append("\n  sites:\n")
    for m in ctx.opt_misses {
        at :: "${m.span.start.file}:${m.span.start.line}:${m.span.start.column}"
        out.append("    [${at}] ${m.pass} — ${m.note}\n")
    }
    return out.to_string()
}
